4. Accessibility Evaluation

Accessibility was assessed using automated axe-core scans and Lighthouse audits.

The implementation was evaluated against WCAG 2.0 and WCAG 2.1 Level A and AA rule sets.

Accessibility requirements
Area	Expected implementation
HTML structure	Appropriate semantic elements for page sections
Images	Alternative text for meaningful images
Forms	Associated labels and identifiable controls
Keyboard focus	Visible focus indicators
Navigation	Interactive elements accessible through keyboard input
Contrast	Sufficient contrast between foreground and background
Controls	Descriptive names and accessible interaction states
Automated accessibility results

axe-core scans covered nine application pages in both light and dark themes. Checkout was additionally included when reached through the keyboard shopping scenario.

No axe violations were recorded in the supplied test results.

Lighthouse accessibility measurements:

Page	Desktop	Mobile
Catalog	100	100
Login	100	100

The overall Lighthouse report also shows an accessibility score of 93/100 for the desktop overview.

A perfect Lighthouse score on individual pages does not independently demonstrate full WCAG compliance. Manual assistive-technology testing remains necessary for a complete accessibility assessment.

5. Keyboard Navigation

The primary purchasing journey was tested without using mouse interaction.

Tested navigation sequence
Open the Catalog page using keyboard navigation.
Move through the available products using Tab.
Open a product with Enter.
Navigate to the Add to Cart control.
Open the shopping cart.
Continue to Checkout.
Navigate to Login when authentication is requested.
Return to the checkout process.
Enter the required delivery information.
Submit the order and reach the confirmation page.
Keyboard test results
Browser engine	Desktop	Mobile
Chromium	✓ PASS	✓ PASS
Firefox	✓ PASS	✓ PASS
WebKit	Not verified	Not verified

The WebKit keyboard test was skipped because the automated environment did not consistently move focus through links using its keyboard-navigation mechanism. A separate control test reproduced this behavior with standard HTML elements.

This result is classified as a testing-environment limitation rather than a confirmed application failure. Additional verification in Safari with its keyboard accessibility settings enabled is recommended.

The test does not cover every possible keyboard interaction, including all reverse-navigation paths, dropdown behaviors, and screen-reader announcements.

6. Lighthouse Evaluation

The supplied Lighthouse screenshots show the following overall desktop results:

Category	Score
Performance	100/100
Accessibility	93/100
Best Practices	100/100
SEO	92/100

The mobile screenshots also show a Best Practices score of 100 and an SEO score of 92.

SEO observation

The SEO audit identified an invalid robots.txt resource, with 22 errors reported by Lighthouse.

This should be investigated because crawler access and indexing configuration can affect how search engines discover website content.

The report does not establish that the website is already indexed or that all search-engine optimization requirements are satisfied.

Lighthouse interpretation

Performance and Best Practices received full scores in the supplied desktop overview. SEO requires additional attention because of the reported robots.txt issue.

These measurements represent the pages and conditions captured during the Lighthouse runs and should not be treated as permanent scores for every route.

